<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anglo-Persian War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a>–<a href="/years/1857">1857</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Anglo-Persian War was fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> from November 1856 to April 1857, after Persia occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. British and Indian forces landed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bushehr">Bushehr</a> and won at Khushab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorramshahr">Mohammerah</a>. By the Treaty of Paris of 1857 Persia withdrew from Herat and renounced its claims there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anglo-persian-war.jpg" alt="Anglo-Persian War" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Koosh-Ab_Battle_Persia_British_cavalry_charge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-great-game">Great Game</a>, the Anglo-Russian contest for influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, the British wanted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> to remain independent as a buffer state against Russian expansion towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. They opposed Iranian influence there because they thought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> unduly influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Iran, ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar dynasty</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> on 25 October 1856, in violation of an existing Anglo-Persian treaty.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> had declared itself independent under its own rebellious emir and placed itself under British protection. The British governor-general in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, acting on orders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, declared war on 1 November. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> army listed 86,700 men on paper, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> could mobilise no more than 20,000, and morale was low in all but a few Imperial Guard regiments.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The first division of the expedition landed near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bushehr">Bushehr</a> on 5 December 1856, stormed the old fort at Reshire and, after a naval bombardment, took the city. Since 4,000 Iranian troops were at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, the division was too weak to go inland, and a second division came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in late January 1857 under Sir James Outram of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> army. On 8 February 1857 Outram defeated the Iranian army of 7,000 to 8,000 under Shoja ol-Molk at Khushab, where the 3rd Bombay Light Cavalry broke into the enemy infantry.</p><p>The British then moved up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatt-al-arab">Shatt al-Arab</a> with 1,500 British and 2,400 Indian soldiers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorramshahr">Mohammerah</a>, at the junction with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karun-river">Karun River</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> and the 64th Foot and 78th Highlanders took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahvaz">Ahvaz</a> on 1 April 1857.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A peace treaty was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 4 March 1857, though news reached the force only on 4 April, when Outram was planning an invasion of the interior. The Shah agreed to withdraw from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, refrain from interfering in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, apologise to the British ambassador, sign a commercial treaty and cooperate in suppressing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>. The British dropped their demand that the Iranians replace their prime minister and agreed not to shelter the Shah's opponents in their embassy.</p><p>The British left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bushehr">Bushehr</a> in October, and the troops were soon needed in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857">Indian Rebellion of 1857</a>. Three Victoria Crosses were awarded for the expedition. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> returned to more direct Afghan control when Dost Mohammed Khan retook it in 1863.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anglo-Persian War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
